WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.110 --> 00:00:04.620
Speaker 1: We have not really talked about
COVID for a while because it's become kind

00:00:04.620 --> 00:00:08.969
of background noise to so many people, whether
that's right or wrong, I'm not saying.

00:00:08.969 --> 00:00:13.619
But it has become background noise to a large
share of the United States.

00:00:13.619 --> 00:00:20.200
But we're currently in the middle of a wave
so massive that the real number of infections

00:00:20.200 --> 00:00:25.700
might be 500,000 infections per day already.

00:00:25.700 --> 00:00:26.960
Already now.

00:00:26.960 --> 00:00:29.480
And you might say, well, but hold on a second,
David.

00:00:29.480 --> 00:00:31.550
Those aren't the official numbers right now.

00:00:31.550 --> 00:00:33.570
And you would be absolutely correct.

00:00:33.570 --> 00:00:35.390
But I'll explain.

00:00:35.390 --> 00:00:41.650
If you look at the official case numbers,
you'll see that we're back up to about 100,000

00:00:41.650 --> 00:00:42.650
cases per day.

00:00:42.650 --> 00:00:48.970
In fact, if I look at the absolute newest
numbers, we're at about 99,500 cases per day.

00:00:48.970 --> 00:00:51.920
So call it 100,000 cases per day.

00:00:51.920 --> 00:00:57.780
But we're now at a point where there's just
a ton of sort of like who cares around the

00:00:57.780 --> 00:00:59.059
country?

00:00:59.059 --> 00:01:04.960
And the belief is that we're capturing maybe
only one out of five actual infections that

00:01:04.960 --> 00:01:05.960
we have.

00:01:05.960 --> 00:01:10.960
Now, remember, a case is you've got a positive
test, an infection is someone has the virus,

00:01:10.960 --> 00:01:13.670
whether it shows up as a positive test or
not.

00:01:13.670 --> 00:01:18.280
And there's a few different reasons that we
are not capturing nearly all of the infections.

00:01:18.280 --> 00:01:22.500
Number one, many people are just no longer
getting tested when they have symptoms.

00:01:22.500 --> 00:01:24.150
Oh, I've got symptoms.

00:01:24.150 --> 00:01:25.479
It's probably COVID or not.

00:01:25.479 --> 00:01:26.479
I don't know.

00:01:26.479 --> 00:01:27.510
But I'm just staying home.

00:01:27.510 --> 00:01:32.240
Or maybe I'm not even staying home if you're
less responsible or unable to.

00:01:32.240 --> 00:01:34.860
But that's an infection and it's not showing
up as a case.

00:01:34.860 --> 00:01:36.680
That's that's first and foremost.

00:01:36.680 --> 00:01:39.680
Number two, we have a ton of people getting
home tests.

00:01:39.680 --> 00:01:42.140
And these home tests, there's nothing magical.

00:01:42.140 --> 00:01:46.350
There's no Wi-Fi, Bluetooth or RFID in your
home test.

00:01:46.350 --> 00:01:51.009
If you test positive on a home test and you
don't report it anywhere, that also doesn't

00:01:51.009 --> 00:01:54.210
show up in the case numbers, even though it's
an infection.

00:01:54.210 --> 00:01:59.909
And then thirdly, there's essentially no more
testing of asymptomatic people going on very

00:01:59.909 --> 00:02:00.909
sparingly.

00:02:00.909 --> 00:02:05.650
There are places that are doing surveillance
testing, meaning everybody gets tested on

00:02:05.650 --> 00:02:10.520
some period of time, friends of mine and family
who work in academic settings.

00:02:10.520 --> 00:02:16.349
Some of them say, yeah, we get tested once
a week or twice a week just to keep the campus

00:02:16.349 --> 00:02:17.920
sort of in a regular testing mode.

00:02:17.920 --> 00:02:19.940
But that's basically gone away.

00:02:19.940 --> 00:02:25.060
And so asymptomatic people are essentially
not showing up anywhere.

00:02:25.060 --> 00:02:30.830
And so to get the real number of infections,
we have to multiply by roughly five, says

00:02:30.830 --> 00:02:34.270
Dr. Eric Topol, who has a great Twitter on
COVID stuff.

00:02:34.270 --> 00:02:40.180
And he's been a guest on this show, meaning
that our 100,000 official cases per day probably

00:02:40.180 --> 00:02:47.569
means around half a million actual infections
per day, which is just an incredible number.

00:02:47.569 --> 00:02:54.860
Now, the good news so far is that if we look
at deaths, deaths continue to decline.

00:02:54.860 --> 00:03:00.769
And we typically believe there's about a three
or four week delay from when cases increase

00:03:00.769 --> 00:03:03.129
before you see an increase in deaths.

00:03:03.129 --> 00:03:10.400
And it's been two months of increasing cases
and deaths continue to decline now down technically

00:03:10.400 --> 00:03:15.480
below the level of flu deaths in the United
States, in the sense that if in the average

00:03:15.480 --> 00:03:18.340
oh, I'm sorry, no, that's completely wrong,
but I apologize.

00:03:18.340 --> 00:03:19.560
That's not true at all.

00:03:19.560 --> 00:03:24.220
We average about a thousand flu I'm sorry,
a hundred flu deaths a day in an average flu

00:03:24.220 --> 00:03:27.940
season and word about 250 COVID deaths a day.

00:03:27.940 --> 00:03:33.330
So still two and a half times as many COVID
deaths as flu deaths.

00:03:33.330 --> 00:03:35.480
But hospitalizations are going up.

00:03:35.480 --> 00:03:41.980
So it is very it's conceivable and plausible
that soon we are going to see the death numbers

00:03:41.980 --> 00:03:43.280
go up as well.

00:03:43.280 --> 00:03:46.379
We're already seeing a 20% increase in hospitalizations.

00:03:46.379 --> 00:03:51.709
Now, in the meantime, we are learning many
new things about COVID.

00:03:51.709 --> 00:03:54.650
One of those is not a good piece of news.

00:03:54.650 --> 00:04:02.549
It's that COVID is now evolving quickly enough
with enough variants that are we've had variants

00:04:02.549 --> 00:04:07.310
forever, but now the variants actually seem
to slightly more regularly outcompete each

00:04:07.310 --> 00:04:10.049
other, as Dr. Eric Topol has written about.

00:04:10.049 --> 00:04:17.029
And this means that more quickly we are getting
variants that can break through boosters and

00:04:17.029 --> 00:04:22.580
can break through natural immunity as well
to reinfect people potentially multiple times

00:04:22.580 --> 00:04:23.840
per year.

00:04:23.840 --> 00:04:26.970
That's very, very much not good.

00:04:26.970 --> 00:04:31.460
Now, there is one bit of good news if we want
to look for one, which is that we're currently

00:04:31.460 --> 00:04:37.589
